<FoundationLayout
  numeral="VII"
  eyebrow="Foundation VII"
  title="Three Named Together"
  tagline="Why naming the Father, the Son and the Spirit in one sentence makes no three-in-one God"
  description="Why naming the Father, the Son and the Spirit in one sentence makes no three-in-one God"
>

  <h2>The Argument in One Line</h2>

  <p>
    Some verses in the Apostolic writings name the Father, the Son and the Spirit close together. Paul closes a letter with the favor of the Master Yahushua Messiah, the love of God, and the communion of the Holy Ghost <em>2 Corinthians 13:14</em>. Peter opens a letter with God the Father, the Spirit, and the blood of Yahushua Messiah <em>1 Peter 1:2</em>. There are more than a dozen passages like these.
  </p>

  <p>
    The argument built on them is always the same. Three are named together. They are named side by side. Therefore, it is said, the three must be one God in three persons. This piece answers every one of those passages at once, because every one of them rests on that single step.
  </p>

  <h2>What the Step Assumes</h2>

  <p>
    The step assumes that when Scripture names several together, it is telling the reader they share one nature. But nobody reads any other sentence that way. If a man thanks “my father, my brother, and my teacher,” no one decides the three are one man. Naming several in one breath shows they are joined in one work. It says nothing about what they are.
  </p>

  <p>
    So the real question is not whether three are named. It is what each one is called, what each one does, and who is placed over whom. On every one of those questions the triad verses answer the same way.
  </p>

  <h2>Scripture Names Others the Same Way</h2>

  <p>
    The plainest proof that a list of three makes no Godhead is that Scripture builds the very same lists with men and angels in them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Paul charged Timothy “before God, and the Master Yahushua Messiah, and the elect angels” <em>1 Timothy 5:21</em>. Here is a triad: God, the Son, and the angels. Paul gives all three the same standing in the sentence. Nobody concludes the angels are part of God.
  </p>

  <p>
    John opens Revelation with favor and peace from three sources: from the One “which is, and which was, and which is to come,” from “the seven Spirits which are before his throne,” and from Yahushua Messiah <em>Revelation 1:4–5</em>. If three named together made a three-person God, this verse would make a God of nine.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Master said the Son of man will come “in his own glory, and in his Father’s, and of the holy angels” <em>Luke 9:26</em>. Three glories, named together. Only one of them belongs to God.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Hebrew Scriptures do the same with men. Israel “believed the LORD [Yahuah], and his servant Moses” <em>Exodus 14:31</em>. The people “greatly feared the LORD [Yahuah] and Samuel” <em>1 Samuel 12:18</em>. The whole assembly “worshipped the LORD [Yahuah], and the king” <em>1 Chronicles 29:20</em>. The apostles wrote, “it seemed good to the Holy Ghost, and to us” <em>Acts 15:28</em>. Yahuah and a man are named in one breath, even as the objects of the same belief, fear and bowing. No reader thinks Moses, Samuel or David was part of Yahuah.
  </p>

  <p>
    John names a triad of witnesses that agree: “the spirit, and the water, and the blood: and these three agree in one” <em>1 John 5:8</em>. Three that are one in agreement — one testimony, not one being. That is how Scripture uses “one” for things joined in a work.
  </p>

  <h2>Who Is Called God in the List</h2>

  <p>
    Read any triad verse slowly and look for the word “God.” In nearly every one, the word belongs to one of the three — and it is always the Father.
  </p>

  <p>
    Paul lists “the same Spirit,” “the same Lord,” and “the same God which worketh all in all” <em>1 Corinthians 12:4–6</em>. Three are named, but only one is called God. Paul does it again: one Spirit, one Lord, “one God and Father of all, who is above all” <em>Ephesians 4:4–6</em>. Peter does it: “God the Father,” the Spirit, and Yahushua Messiah <em>1 Peter 1:2</em>. Paul’s closing blessing does it: the favor of the Master, “the love of God,” and the communion of the Holy Ghost <em>2 Corinthians 13:14</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Scripture says “God the Father” again and again <em>Galatians 1:1</em> <em>Philippians 2:11</em>. It never once says “God the Son” or “God the Holy Ghost.” Those phrases were written by the creeds, not by the apostles. The triad verses do not spread the word “God” across three. They pin it on one.
  </p>

  <h2>The Order Never Turns Around</h2>

  <p>
    When the three act together, the roles never trade places. The Father sends; the Son is sent <em>Galatians 4:4</em>. The Father sends forth “the Spirit of his Son” <em>Galatians 4:6</em>. The Son receives the promised Spirit from the Father and pours it out <em>Acts 2:33</em>. The Father is “above all” <em>Ephesians 4:6</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Partners who are equal can swap jobs. These never do. Not once does the Son send the Father, raise the Father, or give the Father anything. Not once does the Spirit send the Father. A list where one is always the source and the others always receive from him is not a list of equals. It is a chain of command, with the Father at the head <em>1 Corinthians 11:3</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Count the Greetings</h2>

  <p>
    If the apostles believed in three equal persons, their greetings would show it. Paul opens his letters with favor and peace “from God our Father, and the Master Yahushua Messiah” <em>Romans 1:7</em> <em>1 Corinthians 1:3</em>. Two are named, every time. Not one of Paul’s opening greetings names the Spirit.
  </p>

  <p>
    John says the same about fellowship: “truly our fellowship is with the Father, and with his Son Yahushua Messiah” <em>1 John 1:3</em>. The Master defined eternal life as knowing “thee the only true God, and Yahushua Messiah, whom thou hast sent” <em>John 17:3</em>. The Father is the only true God; the Son is the one He sent. The Spirit is not named as a third party in either place, because the Spirit is how the Father and the Son are present, not a third one beside them.
  </p>

  <h2>“In the Name”</h2>

  <p>
    One triad verse uses the word “name”: “in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost” <em>Matthew 28:19</em>. Some read the singular “name” as one name shared by three persons. But “in the name of” means on the authority of, the way a messenger comes “in the name of” the one who sent him. And when the apostles actually baptized, Scripture records the name they used: “in the name of Yahushua Messiah” <em>Acts 2:38</em>, “in the name of the Master Yahushua” <em>Acts 8:16</em> <em>Acts 19:5</em>. The name they called was the name of the Son, the one sent by the Father. The singular word does not fuse three into one.
  </p>

  <h2>The Test for Every Triad Verse</h2>

  <p>
    Every verse that names the Father, the Son and the Spirit together can be read with three plain questions.
  </p>

  <p>
    First, who in the list is called God? The answer is the Father. Second, who sends, gives, raises or stands above? The answer is the Father. Third, does Scripture build lists like this with men or angels in them? It does, and no one makes those lists into a God.
  </p>

  <p>
    A verse that names three together tells the reader that the Father, the Son He sent, and the Spirit He pours out are joined in one work of deliverance. It does not tell the reader that the three are one God. That idea has to be brought to the verse from outside, because no triad verse says it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The last pages of the Bible give the final picture. John saw “the throne of God and of the Lamb” <em>Revelation 22:1</em> <em>Revelation 22:3</em>. One throne is God’s, and the Lamb is given a place in it. The Spirit stands before the throne as seven lamps <em>Revelation 4:5</em>, not seated on it. At the very end there is one God on the throne, the Lamb He exalted, and the Spirit sent out from Him into all the earth <em>Revelation 5:6</em>.
  </p>

